/* Wealth Lab · Module 9 Academic Bonds, Funds and Alternatives · 2026-11-02 */
window.WealthLabAcademicM9Data={"schema":"wealth-lab-m9-academic-v1","version":"3.0.0","reviewDate":"2026-11-02","lessonIds":["L9.S1","L9.S2","L9.S3","L9.S4"],"lessons":[{"id":"L9.S1","title":"Bonds and Yield","subtitle":"Promised cash flows, interest-rate sensitivity, credit pricing, portfolio duration and fixed-income claims","prerequisites":["L7.S2 term structures and bond price mathematics","L7.S3 present value and annuities","L8.S2 corporate cash flow and debt"],"objectives":["Separate coupon, current yield, yield to maturity and realized returns","Price fixed coupon and zero coupon cash flows with consistent time conventions","Calculate duration approximations and bond fund stress","Assess credit spreads and issuer capacity to repay","Explain callable, inflation-indexed and floating rate bonds","Design a bond ladder consistent with dated household liabilities"],"sections":[{"title":"The bond contract: cash-flow rights before yield quotes","body":["A bond is a contractual debt claim. The indenture or offering terms identify issuer, face value, coupon mechanism, principal repayment, maturity, seniority, collateral, optional redemption, covenants, payment frequency and possible default procedures. Unlike common equity, the holder ordinarily has a contractual payment claim, but payment is only as reliable as the obligor and any legally effective guarantee. Credit risk is not eliminated by the phrase fixed income. Government, municipal, corporate, mortgage-backed and high-yield bonds have materially different issuer and structural risks.","Cash flow schedules must be built before comparing quoted yields. A $1,000 face bond with a 4% annual coupon pays $40 each year under an annual-payment hypothetical. A real Treasury note typically pays semiannually, so the actual coupon dates, accrued interest and yield convention require adjustment. Market price can differ from par: a buyer might pay $950 to receive $40 cash coupons and eventual $1,000 par if payments are made as promised. A bond's quoted face amount is not its current resale price.","Debtholder priority is specific to the instrument. Secured bonds may have claims to identified collateral; unsecured senior obligations can rank ahead of subordinated debt; preferred stock and common equity have other priorities. Priority does not guarantee recovery, particularly when collateral value falls or legal costs consume proceeds. Review the offering document and issuer filings, not an issuer's brand reputation alone.","For household asset matching, identify when cash must arrive and what happens if the security cannot be sold at a fair price before then. A bond due in twenty years may pay predictable coupons but be unsuitable for cash due at next year's home closing. The goal is reliable, date-matched spendable resources rather than a maximized yield quote."],"worked":["Face value $1,000 and annual coupon rate 4% gives $40 per year under the stated annual-pay example.","If the price is $950, cash current yield=$40/$950≈4.2105%.","The remaining $50 discount to expected face repayment is not included in current yield.","Yield to maturity accounts for coupon timing and principal repayment at a rate equating price and promised flows.","Default, early calls and transaction costs can break the promised-flow calculation."],"practice":[["Why does a bond trading at $950 have a current yield different from its contractual coupon rate?","The coupon rate is $40 divided by $1,000 face value, while current yield is $40 divided by the $950 price actually paid."],["Why is the bond indenture essential even when a bond is advertised with an attractive stated yield?","The indenture sets seniority, collateral, repayment dates, call rights and protections; the same yield can hide very different default and cash-flow risks."]]},{"title":"Price, current yield, yield to maturity and total realized return","body":["Coupon rate, current yield, yield to maturity (YTM) and realized return answer different questions. The coupon rate measures contracted periodic payment relative to face; current yield compares annual cash coupon to the market purchase price; YTM discounts all promised coupons plus principal to the purchase price. YTM ordinarily assumes the issuer performs under the defined terms, and it is not the actual return an investor necessarily realizes upon an early sale or reinvestment of coupons.","The price of an annual-pay bond with coupon C, face F, yield y and n remaining annual payments is P=Σ(C/(1+y)^t)+F/(1+y)^n for t=1 to n. If $1,000 face pays $40 yearly for three years and market required yield is 5%, model price is about $972.77, below face. At a 3% yield, the same contractual cash flows have a higher price. Yield and market price generally move in opposite directions under otherwise unchanged contract and credit assumptions.","A bond purchased for $950 and sold for $960 after one year with $40 coupon generates a simple pretax holding-period return ($960−$950+$40)/$950≈5.263%. That is not YTM because the principal was not held to final scheduled repayment. A fund advertising a yield also has share price and management expenses that affect investors. Comparisons should match total realized cash flow, tax and horizon rather than one quoted yield column.","Quote conventions can introduce more differences: clean versus dirty price with accrued interest, nominal versus effective yield, semiannual convention, call-adjusted yield and zero coupon discount rates. For a bond issued at a discount, tax treatment can include original issue discount under statutory rules. State all calculation conventions alongside a result; precision without these conditions is misleading."],"worked":["$1,000 face annual coupon $40 for three years at 5% discount rate ⇒ PV≈$972.77.","Bond bought at $950, later sold at $960 and pays $40 coupon ⇒ one-year pretax total cash return≈5.2632%.","Current yield on purchase=$40/$950≈4.2105%, distinct from realized return.","YTM would solve all promised future dated cash flows against the $950 acquisition price.","An early sale at a lower quote could produce a negative realized return despite positive coupons."],"practice":[["Why should an investor not treat yield to maturity as a guarantee of realized wealth?","Issuer payment, reinvestment, taxes, trading costs, optional redemption and whether the investor actually holds to maturity can differ from the simplifying YTM premises."],["Why can a bond price fall while its current yield rises?","The contractual cash coupon stays the same while a lower market price makes coupon divided by price larger, often reflecting changed rate or credit risk."]]},{"title":"Duration, convexity and the difference between an individual bond and a fund","body":["Duration captures the market price sensitivity of a stream of cash flows to yield changes. Under a small parallel shock, modified duration D gives ΔP/P≈−D×Δy, with yield change expressed as a decimal. A portfolio with modified duration six and $20,000 market value exposed to a +0.75 percentage-point yield shift has an approximate price loss of 4.5%, or $900 before coupon income, convexity, credit effects and fund flows. The model is not a predicted maximum loss or a guaranteed exact response.","Convexity corrects part of the nonlinear price/yield relationship: a second-order approximation includes ½×convexity×(Δy)^2. Callable bonds and mortgage-backed claims can have changing effective duration and negative convexity because borrowers or issuers can prepay when refinancing becomes favorable. Market stress can also increase bid-ask spreads and impair the ability to sell near a calculated value.","An individual bond held to final maturity with full payment returns its contractual face value, but it can still have issuer default, purchasing-power and opportunity-cost risk. A traditional open-ended bond fund generally rolls holdings to maintain a strategy and has no single date when the investor's original share purchase is repaid at par. Fund NAV can decline as interest rates rise, and selling shares to pay a mortgage closing cost crystallizes the then-current market quote.","Analyze duration together with a liability schedule: money needed in three months belongs in highly liquid short-maturity holdings, not an intermediate-duration bond fund merely because that fund currently yields more. Bonds can still serve a long-run portfolio as diversifiers and cash-flow sources, but the maturity, liquidity and credit exposure should match the household obligation."],"worked":["Bond portfolio $20,000; modified duration six, yield rises 75 bps =.0075.","Approximate percentage price effect −6×.0075=−4.5%.","Approximate price loss $20,000×.045=$900, resulting mark $19,100 before other factors.","A 100-bp yield decrease would produce approximate +6% before convexity under a symmetric linear approximation.","Individual bond maturity cash flows and fund share NAV are different claims."],"practice":[["How does duration six differ from saying the bond is due in six years?","Duration weights discounted cash-flow timing and approximates sensitivity; its years are not simply the legal maturity and can shift with yield and options."],["Why does a bond fund normally not promise to repay each shareholder's purchase price on a fixed maturity date?","A continuing fund has changing holdings and a market-priced net asset value, unlike one specified individual security's promised principal at its scheduled maturity."]]},{"title":"Credit spreads, defaults, recovery and issuer financial capacity","body":["A bond yield contains more than a risk-free time value. Corporate spreads may reflect expected default probability, loss given default, liquidity, tax, optionality and risk premiums. If comparable Treasury yield is 4% and a corporate security's market yield is 6%, a 200-basis-point spread does not guarantee 2% more realized wealth. A downturn may simultaneously raise spreads and reduce credit recovery. The appropriate analysis examines both quoted compensation and the issuer's ability to pay.","A simplified expected-credit-loss calculation is annual default probability × loss-given-default × exposure, though timing and correlated risk make real bond credit pricing more complicated. At an illustrative 3% default probability and 60% loss severity, one-year expected principal loss is 1.8% of exposure. For $10,000 exposure, that is $180 in a stylized one-year model. This is neither a forecast nor a complete risk-adjusted return because risk premiums, maturity and recovery timing matter.","Evaluate interest coverage, net debt, unencumbered assets, maturity ladders, covenant headroom and refinancing access. A firm may report positive net income but lack cash when a large bond issue becomes due. A bond marketed as 'senior' may still have recovery lower than expected when assets are pledged or liabilities are more complex than a brochure indicates. Issuer rating agency grades are opinions about credit, not government guarantees.","Stress total wealth when spreads widen. A corporate bond fund may lose value even when Treasury rates fall, if credit risk spreads increase more. A household relying on cash at closing should avoid assuming a high-yield bond provides the liquidity and certainty of an insured deposit. Research actual offering documents and credit financial statements."],"worked":["Fictional corporate yield 6%, comparable Treasury 4% ⇒ 2 percentage points spread =200 bps.","Simplified probability of default 3% and loss severity 60% ⇒ expected annual loss 1.8% of principal.","On $10,000 exposure, stylized expected loss $180; actual outcomes can include zero loss or far more.","Net carry after expected losses is not reliably 6%−1.8% because risk premium, taxes and market price affect results.","A spread widening can reduce price before a legal default happens."],"practice":[["Why is a 200-basis-point corporate spread not an investment profit guarantee?","It compensates for issuer default, losses, liquidity, options and other priced risks; the actual credit outcome and resale price are uncertain."],["Which statements help test a corporate issuer's bond repayment capacity?","Cash flow, debt maturity schedule, interest expense, balance-sheet collateral, covenants and access to refinancing, read with the actual issue terms."]]},{"title":"Calls, floating rates, inflation-indexed principal and bond structural risk","body":["Bond contracts can change expected cash flows through call provisions, put rights, floating-rate resets, sinking funds and inflation indexation. A callable issuer may redeem early when yields fall, removing the holder's attractive coupon and forcing reinvestment at lower available rates. A floating-rate note may reset with an index and spread, reducing some interest-rate sensitivity but exposing income to future short-rate changes, issuer credit and floor/cap terms.","TIPS adjust Treasury principal under an inflation-index mechanism using CPI; they offer particular maturity protections for eligible individual securities, but their market value can decline before maturity when real yields rise. A TIPS mutual fund can decline with market prices even if the underlying securities adjust principal. Expected inflation, real rates, tax treatment and liquidity all matter. A fixed coupon bond exposes the investor to inflation erosion of purchasing power, even if the issuer pays every stated nominal dollar.","Compare a $1,000 face callable bond with 6% coupon and a call at par in year two. If it is called after two years, annual coupons of $60 are paid for only those years before principal return; the investor does not receive an imaginary decade of 6% coupons simply because the legal maturity was initially ten years. Yield-to-call and yield-to-worst methods can highlight this risk, but actual call decisions remain contingent and market yields can move.","Bond structures should be matched with an investor's goal and tax treatment. Municipals can have complex federal and state tax consequences, including AMT or taxability depending on issues. Taxable US Treasury interest generally avoids state and local income tax, but this tax treatment is separate from credit, maturity and price risk. A tax advantage should not justify concentrating essential home funds in long-duration or poorly understood bonds."],"worked":["Illustrative 6% annual coupon on $1,000 face gives $60 annually.","If called at par after year two, holder gets two modeled $60 coupons plus $1,000 redemption at call, not eight further coupons.","A 5% annual price inflation factor reduces real purchasing power of unchanged $1,000 maturity proceeds over time.","TIPS principal adjusts by index mechanism, but a fund's market NAV can fall before intended withdrawal.","Evaluate yield to call/worst where relevant instead of citing final stated maturity alone."],"practice":[["Why can a callable bond perform worse than its quoted long-maturity coupon seems to promise during falling rates?","The issuer may redeem early, so the investor loses future high coupon cash flows and must reinvest at then-available rates."],["Does a TIPS or floating-rate bond eliminate all interest-rate or liquidity risk?","No. Real yield market repricing, credit structure, reset spreads, sale timing and instrument-specific terms remain material."]]},{"title":"Tax-adjusted fixed income and state-level household choices","body":["Taxable-equivalent yield compares taxable and tax-exempt investments under a specified investor tax rate and correct legal exemptions. If a fictional municipal pays 3% federal tax-exempt income and a comparable fully taxable instrument pays 4%, a 22% marginal federal tax rate yields 4%×(1−.22)=3.12% on the taxable instrument, before state tax, NIIT and other rules. Under those stated assumptions the 4% fully taxable yield has slightly higher federal after-tax income. The municipal may also face credit, market and state tax risks; nominal after-tax yield alone is not enough.","Interest on US Treasury bills, notes and bonds is generally taxable federally but exempt from state and local income taxes. Bank CD interest is generally federal taxable income and may face state income tax. Municipal bond interest can be federally exempt on qualifying issues yet not always exempt from a given state's tax. A Connecticut household should verify actual tax status rather than classify every product with the word Treasury or municipal as uniformly untaxed.","Interest, premiums, discounts, original issue discount and capital gains can have different reporting. A bond bought at a large secondary discount can produce tax consequences beyond coupon interest, and Treasury inflation adjustments may produce reporting before cash is received in some taxable-account situations. Use IRS Publication 550, TreasuryDirect tax guidance and a qualified tax professional when dealing with specific issues.","Choose a security only after confirming the date cash is needed, the source of payment, expected tax treatment, yield convention, credit risk and marketable sale conditions. A home closing goal should not be subordinated to speculative attempts to improve after-tax return by a few basis points."],"worked":["Fully taxable bond yields 4%; marginal federal rate hypothetically 22% ⇒ simplified after-federal-tax 3.12%.","Qualifying municipal yields 3% federal exempt under assumptions; taxable-equivalent yield=3%/(1−.22)≈3.846%.","The 4% fully taxable offer exceeds the federal taxable-equivalent 3.846% under this simplified comparison.","State taxes, issuer credit, maturity and market liquidity could reverse the economic ranking.","Treasury interest generally is federal-taxable but exempt from state and local income taxation."],"practice":[["What does the taxable-equivalent yield of a municipal bond measure?","The pretax yield a fully taxable bond would need to match the municipal's assumed after-federal-tax interest income at the specified investor tax rate."],["Why does federally tax-exempt interest not automatically make a municipal bond the safest cash equivalent?","Issuer default, market value, liquidity, alternative minimum tax or state tax treatment and call terms may still make the investment unsuitable."]]},{"title":"Bond ladder and issuer-specific fixed-income investment policy","body":["A ladder holds individual bonds or certificates with staggered maturity dates, aiming to make principal available when planned expenses arrive and to spread reinvestment timing. The design does not remove default or inflation risk; buying too long a maturity for a known obligation still creates sale-price exposure. A practical maturity-matching plan marks emergency funds as immediately available, a three-month expense fund as dated liquid resources, and medium-term future cash as laddered claims with appropriate issuer and account protection.","Suppose a Connecticut family expects $12,000 closing cash in a year and $6,000 emergency reserve immediately. A proposed plan holding all $18,000 in an intermediate bond fund at duration six creates price uncertainty on the whole balance. A +1 percentage point parallel yield shock approximates a 6% or $1,080 price decline before fund income and other factors. An alternative separate immediately liquid reserve and one-year suitable maturity instrument reduces rate exposure, though still requires exact instrument and account review.","Bond-ladder modeling should include purchase price, face amount due, coupon payment schedule, accrued interest and any maturity default, plus reinvestment options. A $1,000 face bill purchased for $980 and paid at $1,000 maturity has $20 interest at maturity under simplified assumptions. That is $20/$980≈2.0408% over the holding period, not automatically an annual 2.0408% yield unless the holding period is a year. Compare quoted bank APY and government bill auction yield only after converting conventions.","The final fixed-income policy documents issuer limits, maturity dates, taxes, inflation, credit spreads, account protections, a no-forced-sale rule and the exact role of each holding. It should clearly distinguish a ladder of individual debt contracts from a continuously reinvesting pooled bond fund. Students must explain why a higher advertised coupon is not enough to override a near-term cash deadline."],"worked":["Cash target $12,000 due in 12 months and emergency reserve $6,000 available immediately.","Hypothetical duration-six bond fund for entire $18,000, +100 bps yield shock ⇒ linear mark decline 6% or $1,080.","Bill bought $980 redeeming $1,000 ⇒ $20 holding-period interest if paid.","Holding-period gain $20/$980≈2.0408%; annualization requires exact time to maturity.","Document near-term access and issuer payment instead of assuming quoted fund yield becomes cash."],"practice":[["What is the principal planning advantage of aligning a bond's maturity to a known expense date?","A maturity-matched payment can reduce the need to sell at an unfavorable interim market price, subject to credit, terms and account availability."],["Why is a ladder of individual maturities not the same claim as ownership of a bond mutual fund?","Individual holdings promise security-specific cash dates, while fund investors own fluctuating portfolio shares and do not receive par of their original investment at a single predetermined date."]]}],"sourceRefs":[["SEC Investor.gov Bond Basics and Risk","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/bonds-or-fixed-income-products/bonds"],["TreasuryDirect Understanding Pricing","https://www.treasurydirect.gov/marketable-securities/understanding-pricing/"],["TreasuryDirect TIPS","https://www.treasurydirect.gov/marketable-securities/tips/"],["FINRA Bonds and Fixed Income","https://www.finra.org/investors/investing/investment-products/bonds"],["IRS Publication 550","https://www.irs.gov/publications/p550"],["TreasuryDirect Tax Forms","https://www.treasurydirect.gov/marketable-securities/tax-forms-and-withholding/"]],"assessment":{"title":"Fixed-income claims, yield conventions and mortgage cash policy","prompt":"Price a three-year $1,000 face annual-coupon bond at 5% discount, compute current yield on a $950 purchase, one-year return on sale at $960, duration-six exposure under +75 bps, and stylized corporate expected loss given a 3% default probability and 60% severity. Compare federal after-tax yields on taxable and exempt instruments. Build a $12,000 home-closing plus $6,000 emergency liquidity ladder with explicit maturities and issuer limits. Critique callable and fund exposures using primary documents.","rubric":["Contract and yield-definition correctness (20%)","Bond price and duration mathematics (25%)","Credit, optionality and liquidity stress (25%)","Tax and maturity-matched cash design (20%)","Sources and scenario transparency (10%)"]}},{"id":"L9.S2","title":"Savings, CDs and Treasuries","subtitle":"Deposit insurance, liquidity ladders, after-tax yield, contractual penalties and cash-preservation engineering","prerequisites":["L7.S1 inflation-adjusted savings","L7.S3 nominal, effective and periodic yields","L9.S1 Treasury yield and bond cash flows"],"objectives":["Classify insured deposits and uninsured securities correctly","Apply FDIC and NCUA deposit categories and limits","Compare CD early-withdrawal penalties with Treasury bill market risk","Compute after-tax and after-inflation yields consistently","Model dated cash ladders without treating reinvestment rates as guaranteed","Build a home-purchase and emergency liquidity policy"],"sections":[{"title":"The purpose of cash: meet obligations when markets do not cooperate","body":["Cash and near-cash holdings are tools for meeting contractual expenses, not merely low-return investments. A household may need rent or mortgage payments, a car repair, an emergency deductible or closing funds on a fixed date. The cost of being unable to pay a required bill can exceed years of small interest-rate differences. Evaluate each instrument by legal ownership, insured amount, access date, withdrawal restrictions, issuer credit, settlement and after-tax return.","An emergency reserve should remain spendable during bank business-hour, transfer or settlement delays. Even an FDIC-insured deposit account can be operationally inaccessible for a short period during a technical outage, account freeze or branch disruption. Keep access redundancy and understand ACH hold times. A TreasuryDirect security can be secure against US Treasury default under customary assumptions yet not immediately transferrable to cash without following its custody and sale rules.","Distinguish principal preservation from fixed market quote. An insured savings account generally maintains nominal deposit balance, subject to insured limits and contract terms; a security sold before maturity changes price. A money market fund seeks a stable share value in many structures but is an investment security, not an FDIC-insured deposit. A household that calls every high-yield product 'savings' risks misunderstanding withdrawals during a market shock.","Model obligations across two different horizons. A $6,000 emergency target belongs in accessible funds, while a $12,000 home closing requirement twelve months out can be scheduled with appropriately maturing claims. A $250 monthly savings capacity does not automatically fund an extra $6,000 closing gap in twelve months, because contributions total only $3,000 before returns. The plan must solve the required cash rather than assume a favorable quoted APY makes up the difference."],"worked":["Emergency reserve required immediately $6,000; future closing cash $12,000.","Available cash $15,000 ⇒ simple funding gap $3,000 if both goals must be funded now.","At $250/month for twelve months contributions total $3,000 before interest or taxes.","A fixed-income market price loss just before closing could reopen that gap.","Liquidity can be worth more than marginal expected yield on a date-sensitive expense."],"practice":[["Why is a Treasury security with a known maturity different from an instantly accessible savings balance?","The Treasury's scheduled payment happens at a specified time, while early sale or transfer depends on custody, settlement and prevailing market price."],["What error results from funding a twelve-month nonoptional down payment with a risky investment's hoped-for return?","The household may have too little cash at closing when prices fall, making expected return an unsuitable substitute for required liquidity."]]},{"title":"FDIC coverage by depositor, bank and ownership category","body":["Federal Deposit Insurance Corporation (FDIC) deposit coverage generally protects eligible deposits at insured banks up to $250,000 per depositor, per insured bank, per applicable ownership category. Checking, savings, money market deposit accounts and CDs are deposit products within eligible categories. The FDIC aggregates balances at the same bank in the same ownership category; simply opening two savings accounts under the same owner's category does not double insured limits. Brokerage securities, mutual funds and crypto products are not covered just because offered through a bank website.","A fictional single person holds $190,000 savings and $100,000 CDs in their sole ownership category at the same FDIC bank. Under the simple standard rule, aggregated deposits equal $290,000 and excess above the $250,000 insured limit is $40,000. Moving a portion to a separately insured bank may change coverage if both accounts remain properly titled. Joint, trust and other ownership categories have additional requirements that must be independently verified, not presumed.","FDIC coverage addresses insured-bank failure and certain insured deposits; it does not guarantee a bank's quoted investment advisory service, prevent inflation losses, or remove CD penalties for contractually voluntary early withdrawal. Check the institution using the FDIC BankFind directory and review current coverage calculations rather than trust a financial advertisement. FDIC pass-through deposit coverage arranged through fintech products may depend on actual bank deposit placement and maintained records.","For substantial balances, use the FDIC's official Electronic Deposit Insurance Estimator (EDIE) and list exact bank legal identities, ownership, beneficiaries and account categories. A family trust can change titling and eligibility, but it is unsafe to promise unlimited protection by adding beneficiary names without verifying current regulatory rules."],"worked":["Single-owner savings $190,000 and CD $100,000 at same FDIC-insured bank.","Same ownership category aggregate $290,000.","Standard eligible insured capacity $250,000 ⇒ excess $40,000 in this simplified case.","Opening another individually owned savings account at the same bank does not increase the standard limit.","A separately insured bank may provide a separate applicable standard limit if account requirements are met."],"practice":[["What is wrong with claiming two separate $200,000 personal savings accounts at the same bank are always each fully FDIC insured?","The FDIC aggregates eligible deposits held by the same depositor in the same category at that insured bank; account count alone does not create new coverage."],["Does FDIC insurance protect an ETF purchased from a brokerage division affiliated with an insured bank?","No. Securities are not bank deposits merely because a bank group or bank website provides the distribution service."]]},{"title":"Credit unions and brokerage money market products are different legal structures","body":["Federally insured credit union shares can have National Credit Union Share Insurance Fund coverage administered by NCUA, generally using a $250,000 standard maximum under applicable membership and ownership categories. The financial instrument is a qualifying credit-union deposit or share account, not every investment sold by a credit union. Confirm the institution's current NCUA coverage and whether special ownership categories apply. State-chartered credit unions may have differing arrangements that should be verified rather than assumed federally insured.","A money market deposit account (MMDA) is a bank account potentially covered by FDIC insurance when held at an eligible institution and within limits. A money market mutual fund (MMF) is a pooled security under investment-company regulations. Some MMFs seek to maintain a stable share value; others, especially certain institutional or prime categories, have distinct liquidity, NAV and redemption provisions. No share class should be labeled deposit-insured simply because its displayed yield resembles savings-account APY.","Securities Investor Protection Corporation (SIPC) protection at a qualifying brokerage responds under specified conditions to missing securities or cash when a brokerage firm fails, subject to limits and eligibility; it does not insure an ETF or fund against stock, bond or other investment losses. This is distinct from FDIC/NCUA deposit insurance and US Treasury obligations. A brokerage sweep deposit program can involve real bank deposits placed at partner banks, requiring exact bank and ownership verification.","An advisor should draw the custody chain: household account, broker or bank, underlying institution or fund issuer, legal title, regulatory protections and redemption procedure. The same mobile banking interface may display a federally insured deposit beside an uninsured stock mutual fund; user interface proximity does not change legal status."],"worked":["A federally insured credit union qualifies for NCUSIF rules subject to ownership and eligibility.","An insured bank MMDA and a money market mutual fund can display comparable yields but different protection.","A $20,000 mutual fund declining 2% becomes $19,600 before costs, despite the account being held by a reputable brokerage.","SIPC does not reverse ordinary market declines or replace FDIC deposit coverage.","Verify actual sweep bank placement before assuming combined coverage across institutions."],"practice":[["Why is a money market deposit account different from a money market mutual fund?","An MMDA is an eligible bank deposit when legal conditions apply, while an MMF is an investment company security without ordinary deposit insurance."],["Why does SIPC protection not guarantee the market value of a bond mutual fund purchased through a brokerage?","SIPC addresses specified missing customer assets in qualifying broker failures, not losses caused by changing security prices or fund portfolios."]]},{"title":"Certificate of deposit math, early withdrawal and reinvestment exposure","body":["A CD is a deposit with contractual term, interest convention, account insurance conditions and specified early-withdrawal terms. Fixed-rate CDs lock the contract rate under their conditions, while variable or callable CDs can differ. Brokered CDs may be resold at fluctuating secondary market prices and involve transaction spreads; the investor may not have the same simple early-withdrawal provisions as a directly issued retail bank CD. Read the issuer, custody, insurance and withdrawal agreements before treating a brokered CD as ordinary savings.","Compare a $10,000 CD with stated 4% effective APY and a twelve-month term. Assuming one-year effective accrual without other charges, ending value is $10,400 before taxes. If withdrawn early when interest earned under a simpler illustration is $200 and the contract penalty is 90 days of approximate interest at a 4% annual simple rate, penalty would be about $98.63 using 90/365 timing. Remaining accrued interest may be only $101.37 before taxes. Actual penalty language and compounding can differ materially.","An interest rate lock creates reinvestment risk: the money might mature when new deposits earn less, while a fixed rate may feel unattractive if rates rise. A short CD ladder spreads reset dates but cannot promise future yields. An early-withdrawal penalty can exceed earned interest and reduce principal under some contract terms. Certain no-penalty CDs provide more flexible access in exchange for different offered rates or conditions.","CD comparisons must use the same after-tax basis, insurance category, penalty horizon and APY calculation. A rate difference of 0.25 percentage points on $5,000 is only $12.50 annually before tax in a simple one-year approximation; risking a required mortgage payment to gain that amount may be irrational. The most suitable CD can be the one whose maturity and access rules best match a known expense."],"worked":["Illustrative $10,000 CD with 4% effective annual yield for one year ⇒ $10,400 before tax.","Hypothetical midterm earned interest $200; 90-day penalty modeled at $10,000×4%×90/365≈$98.63.","Net earned interest after hypothetical penalty ≈$101.37, before tax and actual contract rules.","APY and penalty conventions should be aligned with the issuer's binding agreement.","Rate uncertainty begins again at CD maturity; future yields cannot be assumed."],"practice":[["Why can a brokered CD sold before maturity lose principal despite being issued by an FDIC-insured bank?","Secondary sale price may decline with rates or spreads; deposit insurance for eligible issuer failure differs from market-price protection on a voluntary sale."],["How should a consumer decide whether a CD early-withdrawal penalty is acceptable?","Model exactly when money might be needed, how the contract computes the penalty, whether principal can be reduced and the available accessible reserve elsewhere."]]},{"title":"Treasury bills, note cash flows and settlement-specific liquidity","body":["Treasury bills are short-term US government obligations commonly sold at a discount to face and redeemed at par at maturity, subject to auction terms. TreasuryDirect currently lists standard bills with maturities between four and fifty-two weeks, including several intermediate week terms; auction schedules and available instruments can change. A buyer paying $9,800 for a bill with $10,000 face receives a modeled $200 at maturity, not $200 coupon checks along the way. A bill's investment rate, discount rate and holding-period return use different day-count and denominator conventions.","On the hypothetical $9,800 purchase redeemed at $10,000 after 26 weeks, simple holding-period gain is $200/$9,800≈2.0408%. A crude 52/26 annualization of that simple period return gives approximately 4.0816%, while compounding the half-year period factor twice gives roughly 4.123%. Actual auction pricing uses specified day count and yield conventions; a live market quote cannot be inferred from the example.","Direct Treasury securities are generally subject to federal income tax on interest but exempt from state and local income tax. Interest on bank CDs may be treated differently for state purposes. A Treasury security held through a broker may be sold in a secondary market with market-price risk; a TreasuryDirect-held security may require transfer steps before a pre-maturity sale. The high certainty of scheduled federal payment does not mean every custody channel offers immediate cash any day.","An effective cash-management analysis includes bill auction and settlement dates, maturity dates, reinvestment instructions, federal withholding, tax reports and bank transfer cutoffs. If a homeowner must bring a wire to closing by a particular date, a T-bill maturing afterward is not funded cash even when its promised payment is sound."],"worked":["Bill purchase $9,800, redemption $10,000 ⇒ $200 interest if held and paid as stated.","26-week simple holding-period return $200/$9,800≈2.0408%.","Doubling that period rate gives rough annual simple 4.0816%; compound two equal periods ≈4.123%.","Actual Treasury bill investment and discount quoted yields use specified conventions and time bases.","Interest generally subject to federal, not state/local income tax under applicable Treasury rules."],"practice":[["Why does a 26-week bill's 2.04% holding-period gain not mean the quoted annual yield is exactly 2.04%?","Holding-period and annualized measures use different periods, day counts and quote conventions; convert before comparing to bank APY."],["What operational error can prevent a Treasury bill from funding a planned house closing even without default?","The redemption or sale proceeds may settle or become transferable after the required wire date; custody and maturity timing are crucial."]]},{"title":"After-tax, real cash returns and inflation loss","body":["Nominal deposit growth differs from spendable purchasing power. Model gross interest first, subtract legally applicable tax on that interest, then adjust resulting cash for inflation over the same period. For a simplified $10,000 account earning 4% taxable annual interest, interest is $400. At a hypothetical marginal income tax rate of 22%, modeled federal tax is $88, leaving $312 net interest and $10,312 nominal cash. At 3% annual inflation, real value is $10,312/1.03≈$10,011.65, only slightly above the initial $10,000 of spending power.","Do not subtract the tax rate directly from the principal as if a 22% income tax consumes 22% of the whole bank balance. Taxes often apply to the earned interest, and some investments have different timing, account wrapper or exemption rules. A Treasury security's federal and state tax mix can alter the ranking compared with a CD even when quoted pretax yields are close. Real return comparisons require consistent units, dates and jurisdiction.","A $10,000 cash balance earning zero for one year under 5% inflation has purchasing-power equivalent $10,000/1.05≈$9,523.81. Cash can therefore lose real purchasing power even when nominal deposit principal is safe. That does not automatically imply the household should use volatile equities for emergency money: the cost of a timed funding shortfall may be larger than slow real erosion. Different assets solve different types of risk.","Introduce a two-objective policy: protect enough immediately accessible nominal cash for unexpected bills, while investing long-horizon surplus in suitably diversified assets for potential growth. Test an adverse inflation case and a job-loss case. An accessible reserve is a service provided to the household and should not be evaluated solely by expected percentage return."],"worked":["$10,000 balance ×4% nominal taxable interest ⇒ $400 annual interest.","At illustrative 22% tax on interest, $88 tax; nominal after-tax balance $10,312.","At 3% inflation, real equivalent $10,312/1.03≈$10,011.65.","At 0% nominal on $10,000, 5% inflation reduces prior-year purchasing power to ≈$9,523.81.","Tax rules and effective inflation rate must be checked for actual products."],"practice":[["Why should income tax on cash-account yield not be calculated as 22% of the entire account principal?","Ordinary interest taxation generally applies to taxable earnings, not automatic tax on previously owned deposit principal."],["How can holding some real-return-negative cash still be a financially rational decision?","Accessible nominal funds prevent forced asset sales, missed bills, penalties and refinancing stress during near-term emergencies, benefits that nominal yield alone cannot measure."]]},{"title":"A cash allocation and documented contingency plan","body":["A complete household liquidity plan divides money by obligation, priority and time. First list bills due before the next paycheck, a minimum emergency reserve and a dated mortgage closing target. Then select legal account types with compatible access: immediately available insured deposits for emergency funds, suitable short-term deposits or maturities for future expenses, and longer-term risk assets only for money that can tolerate drawdowns. Keep actual bank account restrictions and insurance totals documented.","Suppose a person has $18,000 liquid assets and needs $6,000 ongoing emergency cash plus $12,000 potential closing within twelve months. No spare risk capital remains from that $18,000 under the basic segmentation. If the person saves an additional $250 monthly, twelve contributions total $3,000, but this new money should not be casually counted both as a future closing contribution and as extra permanent risk investing. If a $2,500 car repair occurs, recalculate both buffers.","Build an allocation grid: instrument, institution, legal protection, amount, ownership category, interest rate convention, first available date, early access cost, estimated tax and matching liability. Verify FDIC/NCUA coverage rather than rely on a badge. Avoid treating a money market mutual fund as a deposit, and do not assume the highest-yielding CD can always be liquidated without a penalty.","The academic output is a cash-defense policy that survives a sudden bank app outage, a three-week job interruption, and interest-rate changes. The policy should specify who can authorize transfers, what happens when a bill matures, and which asset may be sold first without violating the household's essential liquidity floor."],"worked":["Accessible cash $18,000 = emergency allocation $6,000 plus closing allocation $12,000.","Unallocated existing risk capital=$0 under these two stated obligations.","Twelve monthly new $250 contributions=$3,000 before interest.","Unexpected $2,500 deductible reduces aggregate reserves to $15,500 unless replenished.","A plan must rebalance reserves after expenses rather than double count funds."],"practice":[["What is the main error when the same $12,000 is shown as both closing money and a freely investable stock allocation?","It double counts one economic asset against incompatible obligations, ignoring the liquidity required for the planned housing purchase."],["What should be recorded for every deposit or short-term security in the household's cash map?","Legal issuer, account type and owner, applicable protection, restrictions, date funds become available, yield/tax convention and the required expense it funds."]]}],"sourceRefs":[["FDIC Deposit Insurance Basics","https://www.fdic.gov/resources/deposit-insurance/brochures/deposits-at-a-glance"],["FDIC Deposit Insurance FAQ","https://www.fdic.gov/resources/deposit-insurance/faq"],["NCUA Share Insurance","https://ncua.gov/consumers/share-insurance-coverage"],["TreasuryDirect T-bill Facts","https://www.treasurydirect.gov/marketable-securities/treasury-bills/"],["TreasuryDirect Tax Rules","https://www.treasurydirect.gov/marketable-securities/tax-forms-and-withholding/"],["SEC Investor.gov Money Market Funds","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/mutual-funds-and-exchange-traded-funds"],["SIPC What SIPC Protects","https://www.sipc.org/for-investors/what-sipc-protects"]],"assessment":{"title":"Emergency reserve, CD and Treasury cash-flow audit","prompt":"Design a $6,000 immediate emergency and $12,000 twelve-month home-closing cash plan. Audit two deposit balances ($190,000 savings and $100,000 CDs) under basic FDIC single-ownership rules, price a 26-week $9,800-to-$10,000 bill, calculate after-tax and real value of $10,000 interest-bearing savings, model a 90-day CD early-withdrawal penalty and explain how a money market mutual fund differs from a bank MMDA and SIPC coverage. Produce a dated transfer and custody checklist.","rubric":["Insurance and legal product classification (25%)","CD, bill, after-tax and inflation mathematics (25%)","Maturity and liquidity engineering (25%)","Counterparty and custody operational risks (15%)","Sources and verification checklist (10%)"]}},{"id":"L9.S3","title":"ETFs, Mutual Funds and Indexes","subtitle":"Fund ownership, net asset value, diversification, total expenses, tracking and account implementation","prerequisites":["L4.S1 security ownership and fund basics","L8.S1 corporate equities and dilution","L7.S3 compounding and cash-flow return"],"objectives":["Compare index, active, mutual and exchange-traded fund mechanisms","Distinguish NAV, secondary ETF quote and premium/discount","Calculate recurring fee drag and tracking difference","Assess look-through concentration, leverage and underlying exposure","Distinguish fund securities from insured bank deposits","Design a transparent low-cost risk-aware fund allocation"],"sections":[{"title":"The fund as a legal investment pool and the index as a measurement rule","body":["Mutual funds and exchange-traded funds pool investor assets under specific legal investment-company structures and share classes. Investors own shares of the fund, not necessarily a personal legal title to each underlying issuer's stocks or bonds. The fund's prospectus identifies its investment objective, eligible holdings, risks, expenses, redemption mechanism and managers. An index is a rules-based benchmark definition, not a security or a guaranteed investment outcome. An index fund seeks to follow an index through a specified strategy, but may deviate because of costs and implementation.","Indexes differ radically in market scope and weight methodology. Market-cap-weighted, equal-weight, factor, sector and thematic indexes place different concentration on particular issuers and industries. A fund tracking a broad-sounding index may still be dominated by a few large companies or one country. A '500 company' label cannot establish equal diversification; examine holdings by issuer, sector, geography and factor risk. Some funds hold derivatives, futures or synthetic exposures rather than identical direct securities.","An active mutual fund attempts to select securities or exposures to outperform a benchmark or meet another mandate; some ETFs are actively managed and some mutual funds follow indexes. The wrapper does not prove whether a strategy is passive. A fund of funds may layer fees, and some smart beta offerings charge higher prices for systematic factors with uncertain excess performance. The actual prospectus and holdings are more informative than marketing terms.","Begin research with the fund's objective, current statutory prospectus, summary prospectus, annual/semiannual shareholder reports and holdings. Read the benchmark construction methodology and how the fund rebalances. A high historical return can reflect a favorable sector regime, extra risk or luck rather than durable skill. Avoid ranking products on past three-year growth alone when the household's horizon and risk capacity differ."],"worked":["Fund portfolio has $10 million assets and $50,000 liabilities, with one million issued shares.","Simplified net asset value per share=($10m−$50k)/1m=$9.95.","A benchmark index level of 4,000 is not a price at which you can buy all constituents automatically.","A sector fund with 60% holdings concentrated in one industry remains concentrated despite many share positions.","Prospectus costs and exposures determine product suitability, not the wrapper alone."],"practice":[["Does buying a fund tracking a named index guarantee receiving the index's exact published returns?","No. Expenses, transaction costs, sampling, cash holdings, tracking methodology and taxes can make fund performance differ from the benchmark."],["Why might a fund containing hundreds of securities still have significant concentration risk?","Market-cap weighting, overlapping issuers, correlated industries or country/factor exposures can dominate aggregate outcomes despite many holdings."]]},{"title":"Net asset value, ETFs' market prices and creation/redemption mechanism","body":["Fund net asset value (NAV) is generally assets minus liabilities divided by outstanding shares under the fund's valuation rules. Traditional mutual funds generally process qualifying purchases and redemptions at the next calculated end-of-day NAV, while ETFs trade throughout the market session at changing exchange prices. ETF market price can deviate from NAV, particularly when underlying markets are closed, holdings are less liquid or bid-ask spreads widen.","ETF authorized participants can create or redeem large blocks of shares through the fund's specified process, often exchanging baskets of securities and/or cash. Arbitrage incentives can help keep ETF market pricing close to underlying holdings, but do not guarantee a zero premium or continuous liquidity during market stress. A share buyer normally transacts on an exchange with another investor rather than directly with the ETF sponsor. The sponsor's fund holdings and secondary-market trading are related but distinct legal flows.","Suppose a fund's reported NAV is $100 per share and quoted ETF purchase ask is $100.50 while sale bid is $99.90. Buying at $100.50 represents a 0.5% premium to NAV before brokerage costs, and immediately selling at $99.90 realizes a $0.60 spread loss, approximately 0.597% relative to the purchase, even if NAV has not moved. An investor making a small purchase can be affected by spreads more than a few basis points of annual fund expense difference.","Use limit orders and market-session context appropriately. Spreads may widen at the open, close or during underlying market closures; executing through a market order can yield poor realized price. The value of ETF liquidity is the ability to trade at a market-clearing price, not a promise that the spread or premium will be trivial. Compare actual expected trade size, brokerage rules and fund secondary-market depth."],"worked":["Illustrative fund NAV $100, ETF ask $100.50 ⇒ ask premium 0.5%.","Bid $99.90, ask $100.50 ⇒ quoted spread $0.60 per share.","Immediate round-trip loss $0.60/$100.50≈0.597%, ignoring brokerage and NAV changes.","An ETF with low expense ratio can still cost more for short-horizon trading if spreads are wide.","Fund creation/redemption arbitrage is not perfect, especially in stressed markets."],"practice":[["Why can an ETF buyer pay more than the last reported NAV?","The ETF trades at supply/demand-determined secondary-market prices, with spreads, market timing and underlying asset valuation effects; arbitrage only tends to constrain differences."],["Why is a 0.10% expense ratio not the investor's entire ETF cost?","Trading spreads, premium/discount, transaction costs, possible commissions and tax consequences can also affect realized returns."]]},{"title":"Expense ratios, compounding and the difference between gross and net","body":["A fund expense ratio represents certain ongoing annual fund operating expenses as a proportion of assets and is generally reflected in NAV rather than billed as a separate annual invoice to every shareholder. Transaction costs, advisory charges, commissions and some portfolio implementation costs may not appear in the advertised expense ratio. An expensive share class can underperform an otherwise identical low-cost one solely because more capital is consumed by recurring operating fees.","On $20,000, a 0.10% annual fund expense ratio corresponds to approximately $20 of first-year assets and 1% corresponds to approximately $200, with a $180 initial difference. For a deterministic simplified illustration, assume both funds earn 6% before expenses and net annual return factors approximate 1.059 for the 0.10% option and 1.05 for the 1% option. Over 20 years, $20,000 becomes roughly $62,900 versus $53,066; the gap is thousands of dollars from expenses and compounding, under these assumptions.","Be clear about fee timing and mathematical conventions. Simply subtracting annual expense ratio from annual gross return is an educational approximation, not a replica of fund daily net asset value accounting. Actual yields vary year to year, flows differ and fund expense ratios can change. Financial statements and standardized fund performance report net-of-fee historical performance under applicable disclosure rules; avoid subtracting a fee twice when starting from already net figures.","Compare costs at the portfolio level as well as share class. If an advice platform charges 0.50% annually on top of a 0.40% fund operating cost, total illustrative recurring percentage drag can approach 0.90% before other charges, subject to fee methods. A low-cost broad fund may be a practical default for long-horizon diversified exposure, but cost alone does not make an unsuitable asset allocation safe."],"worked":["Initial $20,000; first-year expense at 0.10% ≈$20 versus at 1.00% ≈$200.","Initial expense gap ≈$180/year before future changing account values.","Assume gross 6% annual and simple expense drag ⇒ net rates 5.9% and 5.0%.","After 20 years compare $20,000×1.059^20 with $20,000×1.05^20.","Actual after-fee fund NAV will not evolve in guaranteed smooth rates."],"practice":[["Why do higher annual fund expenses matter more over decades than the first-year fee gap suggests?","Fees reduce the capital that remains invested and can compound in subsequent periods, widening the difference in ending balances under otherwise identical assumptions."],["Why is it incorrect to subtract a fund's expense ratio again from a historical published net return?","Standardized fund historical performance may already reflect stated operating expenses, so subtracting again double counts those charges."]]},{"title":"Tracking error, tracking difference and active-management selection","body":["Tracking difference is the difference between fund and index realized returns over a given horizon, influenced by fees, holdings, cash drag, dividend treatment, securities lending and portfolio rebalancing. Tracking error describes the variability of those differences across observations. The two measures are not interchangeable. An index fund may consistently trail an index by 0.10% per year due to expenses with low tracking error, or vary widely around it while averaging similar performance.","Suppose an index earns 8% in a fictional year and a fund earns 7.7% on a comparable calculation basis. Tracking difference is −0.3 percentage points. If the gap varies from +0.5 points one quarter to −0.7 points another, tracking error requires a properly specified standard deviation of return differences; the simple annual gap alone does not define it. A fund holding foreign securities may experience differences due to local market closes and currency effects.","Active funds should be evaluated net of all relevant fees against an appropriate risk-adjusted benchmark and over a sufficiently informative period, not by cherry-picking a short winning interval. Survivorship bias can make historical surviving-fund leaderboards look overly successful because closed funds disappeared from the comparison. Style drift, excessive turnover and a changing benchmark also undermine conclusions. Past performance does not guarantee future results, and a strong backtest does not prove repeatable stock-picking skill.","The research assignment is to select two plausible funds with the same desired asset exposure, compare underlying holdings, fees, tracking metrics, portfolio turnover, spreads, taxes and fund structure. A valid choice may favor lower fee and simpler exposure while acknowledging implementation tradeoffs. The analyst should describe the exact investor account and expected trading frequency before concluding one ticker is superior."],"worked":["Hypothetical benchmark year return 8.0% and fund year return 7.7%.","Tracking difference fund minus index =−0.3 percentage points.","Tracking error requires a time series of periodic deviations, not just one annual difference.","Historical index methodology and dividends must match fund's reported total-return method.","A winning active fund's one-year result alone is not evidence of persistent skill."],"practice":[["What distinguishes tracking difference from tracking error?","Tracking difference is realized performance gap over a stated horizon, while tracking error measures variability of periodic gaps under a specified statistical method."],["Why does survivorship bias matter in mutual-fund performance rankings?","Defunct or merged unsuccessful funds may be absent from a surviving sample, causing apparent performance history to overstate results investors could have attained."]]},{"title":"Diversification, overlapping funds and factor concentration","body":["Owning two funds does not necessarily double diversification. A total US equity index fund and a large-cap growth fund can share many top holdings and therefore concentrate the same technology-company and valuation exposure. A multi-asset portfolio should look through fund labels to the underlying stocks, bonds, industries, geography, credit duration, currency and factor behavior. The true risk unit is the economic exposure, not the number of ticker symbols in an account.","Illustrate a household with $10,000 fund A and $10,000 fund B where 40% of A and 60% of B overlap in the same group of issuers. If those same overlapping positions constitute the relevant common group, exposure to that group totals $4,000+$6,000=$10,000 or half of the combined portfolio. The total investment may contain hundreds of securities but have concentrated factor risk. Precise issuer-level overlap needs actual holdings data rather than additive category percentages when groups differ.","Asset allocation should follow liabilities and income risk. Broad equity exposure can diversify individual company failures but remains vulnerable to marketwide losses. Bond index funds carry interest-rate and credit duration risk. International funds add currency and foreign regulation. A household whose wages and home equity both depend on the same regional economy may want to avoid further concentrated exposure to that region in investments.","Measure holdings overlap, largest top-ten position weights, region allocation and leverage rather than relying on fund marketing. Rebalance based on explicit risk targets and cash needs. Diversity of wrapper names, such as one ETF and one mutual fund holding similar companies, is not meaningful protection against a correlated selloff."],"worked":["Fund A investment $10,000, overlapping issuer group exposure 40% ⇒ $4,000.","Fund B investment $10,000, same issuer group exposure 60% ⇒ $6,000.","Combined overlapping group exposure $10,000/$20,000=50%.","Fund count two does not establish independent underlying economic risks.","An emergency cash reserve should remain distinct from both equity funds."],"practice":[["Why might adding a large-cap growth ETF to a broad US equity ETF fail to diversify the largest company exposure?","The two funds may share the same top issuers; adding the second can increase concentration in overlapping securities rather than spread economic risk."],["What should an investor inspect to evaluate whether two funds really diversify each other?","Full or representative holdings, issuer weights, asset classes, sector/country mix, duration, leverage and factor correlations, not fund names alone."]]},{"title":"Leveraged, inverse, sector and derivative-based ETFs","body":["Some ETFs are designed for daily leveraged or inverse returns and use derivatives and financing to target multiples of an index's daily move. This daily objective is not a guarantee that holding the fund for months produces the same multiple of the index's multi-month cumulative return. Compounding and path dependence can make performance diverge sharply when prices fluctuate. Fees, borrowing costs, swaps, roll effects and potential counterparty exposures can compound the risks.","Use a two-day example with an index beginning at 100, rising 10% to 110 then declining 9.0909% back to 100. A daily 2× fund, simplifying away fees and tracking deviations, rises 20% on day one, then falls about 18.1818% on day two, leaving 1.20×0.8181818≈0.981818 of starting fund value, a loss of about 1.8182% even though the index returned zero. The fund did not necessarily malfunction; the sequence and reset mechanism created path effects.","Sector ETFs can be narrow while unlevered, and commodity or futures ETFs may have returns affected by futures curves and roll costs rather than just spot prices. An ETF holding a single stock, high-yield credit or illiquid small securities can be far riskier than a broad diversified index fund. An investor should read daily reset objectives, collateral, counterparties and loss exposure. The wrapper 'ETF' is a trading format, not an assurance of safety.","A household seeking home closing money in the next twelve months should not use inverse funds as a simple guaranteed hedge against equity loss without understanding basis, daily reset and timing risks. A sophisticated hedge can fail if the market path differs from an assumed one. Portfolio education should prioritize risk identification before product selection."],"worked":["Index goes 100→110 (+10%) then 110→100 (−9.0909%).","A simple daily 2× leveraged product goes 1.00→1.20 (+20%).","Day two target −18.1818%, ending factor 1.20×(1−.181818)≈.981818.","Index cumulative ≈0%; leveraged product cumulative≈−1.8182% before fees.","Daily target leverage is not equivalent to a fixed multiple of cumulative multi-day return."],"practice":[["Why can a daily 2× ETF lose value over a volatile two-day period while its underlying index returns to its original price?","Daily reset and compounding multiply separate leveraged daily growth factors; volatility and changing percentage denominators can create path-dependent losses."],["Does a passive ETF label guarantee diversified or low-risk investment?","No. An ETF can passively track a highly concentrated, leveraged, inverse or otherwise risky index, with additional transaction and market risks."]]},{"title":"Fund implementation, taxes and a disciplined asset-allocation plan","body":["Fund strategy should begin with the purpose, time horizon and risk budget. Money needed for a near-term housing purchase should not be mixed with long-term retirement investments simply because an app shows them in one account dashboard. Long-horizon assets can support an appropriately diversified allocation consistent with the individual's ability to endure drawdowns, fees and taxes. Investment account type can change tax consequences without changing underlying market volatility.","In taxable brokerage accounts, mutual fund capital-gain distributions and dividend reporting can produce current tax even if the investor has not sold shares. Some ETF structures may be comparatively tax-efficient in some circumstances, but 'ETF' is not synonymous with tax-free. Tax results depend on underlying securities, fund operations, account, investor jurisdiction and actual distributions. Retirement wrappers may defer or exempt specified taxation under their rules but can restrict access before retirement.","Suppose $20,000 long-term capital is split 60% broad equity and 40% high-quality bond index funds. If equity loses 25% and bonds decline 5% simultaneously, model value becomes $12,000×.75+$8,000×.95=$16,600, a $3,400 or 17% portfolio decline. Allocation balances risks; it does not guarantee that bonds offset equities during every shock. A separate $12,000 closing reserve should not be included in these risky amounts.","Create an investment policy listing goal, savings amount, candidate funds, actual benchmarks and holdings, fees, bid-ask spreads, taxes, target allocation, rebalancing threshold and a maximum forced-sale risk. The student must defend a low-cost fund choice with evidence from official prospectuses and independent risk data instead of naming a currently popular ticker."],"worked":["Long-term $20,000 portfolio: $12,000 equity fund and $8,000 bond fund.","Equities decline 25% ⇒ $9,000; bonds decline 5% ⇒ $7,600.","Combined stressed value $16,600; decline $3,400 or 17%.","A separate $12,000 near-term home fund should be held outside the risky portfolio under the scenario.","Historical fund returns and tax efficiencies are not certain future investor outcomes."],"practice":[["What is the main mistake in treating a broad equity ETF as a guaranteed down-payment account?","Even broad equity funds can decline sharply at the time cash is needed; diversification does not remove market risk or guarantee required liquidity."],["Why can an ETF in taxable brokerage generate tax without its owner selling shares?","The fund may distribute taxable dividends or capital gains under its rules, and the investor's tax treatment depends on distributions and jurisdiction."]]}],"sourceRefs":[["SEC Investor.gov Mutual Funds and ETFs","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/mutual-funds-and-exchange-traded-funds"],["SEC Investor.gov ETF Basics","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/exchange-traded-funds-etfs"],["SEC Fund Fees and Expenses","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/mutual-funds-and-exchange-traded-funds/mutual-fund-fees-and-expenses"],["SEC Warning Leveraged and Inverse ETFs","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/general-resources/news-alerts/alerts-bulletins/updated-investor-bulletin"],["FINRA Fund Fees","https://www.finra.org/investors/investing/investment-products/mutual-funds"],["IRS Publication 550","https://www.irs.gov/publications/p550"]],"assessment":{"title":"Fund holdings, fees, ETF market mechanics and household portfolio stress","prompt":"Compute a simple NAV from $10 million assets, $50,000 liabilities and one million shares; compare ETF NAV $100 to ask $100.50 and bid $99.90; evaluate expense ratios 0.10% versus 1.00% on $20,000 over twenty years using hypothetical 6% gross annual return; compute a $20,000 60/40 portfolio loss under −25% equity and −5% bonds. Explain tracking difference versus tracking error, potential fund overlap and path dependence in a two-day daily 2× ETF. Design a risk-aware long-term allocation with separate home-closing cash.","rubric":["Fund ownership, NAV and share creation mechanism (20%)","Expense ratio, return and trading-cost calculations (25%)","Tracking and look-through diversification (20%)","Leveraged instruments and liquidity stress (20%)","Primary disclosures and portfolio-policy clarity (15%)"]}},{"id":"L9.S4","title":"REITs, Annuities and Alternatives","subtitle":"Real-estate security structure, retirement insurance contracts, illiquidity, private investment fees and payout quality","prerequisites":["L2.S2 home equity and real estate costs","L5.S1 insurance cash values and contractual guarantees","L8.S2 cash from operations and leverage","L9.S3 fund fees, share structure and NAV"],"objectives":["Classify equity REITs, mortgage REITs and nontraded REIT liquidity","Separate distributions from income, cash return and return of investor capital","Interpret REIT FFO/AFFO cash-flow metrics with caution","Assess annuitization, guarantees, riders, fees and surrender charges","Model income payout and inflation erosion across a long retirement","Evaluate private-placement disclosure, lockups, leverage and fee waterfalls","Apply household due-date liquidity and trust-beneficiary constraints"],"sections":[{"title":"Public REITs: company shares are real-estate exposure, not deed ownership","body":["A real estate investment trust (REIT) is a company or qualifying vehicle that meets specified federal rules related to real estate holdings, income and shareholder distributions. Equity REITs commonly own income-producing properties and earn rent, while mortgage REITs finance or invest in real-estate debt and may be highly sensitive to leverage and funding spreads. Buying REIT stock generally gives ownership of corporate shares rather than direct title to the underlying building or authority to sell a specific apartment.","Publicly traded REITs offer an exchange-traded market price but remain exposed to equity market volatility, interest rates, tenant vacancies, maintenance, refinancing and management execution. A shopping-center REIT with weak tenant demand can lose property income even while general home values rise. REIT funds offer diversified property-company exposure but can overlap heavily in property sectors or mortgage debt factors; sector labels do not guarantee a stable investment.","Suppose an owner invests $50,000 in a publicly traded REIT and receives $2,000 cash distributions in a year. Cash distribution ratio is $2,000/$50,000=4%, but if the shares' market value falls to $43,000, total before-tax holding-period return is ($43,000−$50,000+$2,000)/$50,000=−10%. A high distribution is not a guaranteed total gain and may not represent recurring property operating profit.","Analyze lease maturities, same-property net operating income, occupancy, debt costs, asset dispositions, tax status and management rights. A property REIT's performance is not a direct substitute for a household's first-home purchase decision: public REIT ownership does not generally provide a place for the household to live or remove mortgage payment obligations."],"worked":["Initial REIT shares $50,000, cash distributions $2,000 ⇒ 4% cash distribution rate.","If market value ends $43,000, price change −$7,000.","Total return ($43,000+$2,000−$50,000)/$50,000=−10% before tax/costs.","A 4% cash distribution can coexist with a negative total investment return.","Shareholder typically lacks direct legal title to individual REIT buildings."],"practice":[["Why is owning REIT shares not legally equivalent to owning a fractional deed to a named apartment building?","The investor owns a security of the REIT entity with corporate rights, not direct title or control over a specified property."],["Why can a REIT's 4% cash distribution coexist with a negative investment year?","The shares can decline more in market value than the cash distributed; distributions alone do not measure total shareholder return."]]},{"title":"REIT property economics, funds from operations and leverage","body":["Traditional net income for a real estate company reflects real estate depreciation, which can be large even when property sale values do not decline in the same pattern. Funds from operations (FFO) is a widely used REIT performance measure under industry conventions that typically adjusts GAAP net income for real-estate depreciation/amortization and certain property sale gains or losses, with details depending on the defined standard. Adjusted FFO (AFFO) has less uniform definition and may address recurring capital expenditure, leasing commissions and straight-line rent effects. Neither metric is universally the same as free cash available to ordinary shareholders.","Take a fictional REIT with GAAP net income $20 million, property depreciation $15 million and excluded qualifying property sale gain $5 million. Under a simplified FFO adjustment, modeled FFO=$20m+$15m−$5m=$30 million. If recurring maintenance and leasing capital demands are $10 million, a simplified available-cash proxy falls to $20 million before other adjustments and debt payments. A $25 million distribution might be below FFO but above this adjusted cash measure; investors need a complete distribution coverage bridge.","Property capitalization rate is net operating income divided by purchase value under defined conditions. An asset producing $6 million property NOI valued at $100 million implies 6% cap rate, not a guaranteed equity yield. If the property finances $60 million debt at a variable rate, rising interest costs can compress equity cash even while property NOI remains stable. Leverage can magnify both property appreciation and losses and can force equity issuance or asset sales when refinancing is difficult.","Review property appraisals, debt maturity ladder, loan covenant ratios, tenant and geographic concentration, fee agreements and recurring capex requirements. If an advisor compares a REIT's advertised cash distribution yield to a bank APY, identify the huge difference in market price, tenant, credit, distribution and liquidity risks before interpreting the percentages."],"worked":["Fictional GAAP net income $20m + property depreciation $15m − property sale gain $5m ⇒ simplified FFO $30m.","Recurring capital/leasing uses $10m ⇒ illustrative AFFO-like cash proxy $20m.","Dividend distributions $25m exceed this $20m proxy by $5m despite being below FFO $30m.","Property NOI $6m divided by property market value $100m ⇒ 6% cap rate.","Cap rate is property-level operating yield before financing, not cash yield to levered REIT shares."],"practice":[["Why can a REIT distribution covered by FFO still be difficult to sustain?","FFO may omit recurring property capital expenditure, leasing costs and some financing needs; after those uses, available cash can be smaller."],["Why does a property's 6% cap rate not guarantee a 6% shareholder return?","Debt interest, vacancies, capital expenditure, management fees, taxes, sale price and financing leverage affect actual cash and equity value."]]},{"title":"Non-traded REITs: redemptions, valuation opacity and principal-funded distributions","body":["Some REIT securities are SEC-registered yet not listed on a public exchange. Their investors may depend on limited issuer redemption programs or an eventual listing, sale or liquidation for liquidity. Redemption programs may impose caps, waiting periods, pricing adjustments or suspension rights. A stated daily or quarterly NAV from sponsor methodology does not necessarily equal a price at which the investor can sell their full position in stress. The SEC's investor bulletins warn particularly about nontraded REIT liquidity, fees and distributions from offering proceeds or borrowing.","A fictional investor pays $50,000 into a non-traded REIT and receives $2,000 annual cash distributions. If $1,200 of the distribution comes from earned property cash and $800 from return of investor capital, the headline 4% cash distribution is only 2.4% from modeled earned cash relative to original principal. Return-of-capital classification can reduce tax basis under applicable rules; it is not newly created investment profit. Actual payout sources and tax reporting require issuer disclosures.","Suppose a redemption agreement only permits 2% of fund shares to be redeemed in a period and requests exceed that amount. A holder asking for $20,000 may receive substantially less or nothing according to program terms. The household cannot treat a redemption request like a bank withdrawal. Sales commissions, organizational expenses, leverage and conflicts involving external managers can further reduce value and align incentives imperfectly.","An investor contemplating a home down payment within two years should not lock the required funds in an asset whose exit depends on a future manager election or uncertain property sale. For any nontraded security, collect the offering circular, fee schedule, risk disclosures, redemption policy, audited statements and related-party transaction descriptions before investing. 'Real estate backed' is not a substitute for a legal claim and liquidity schedule."],"worked":["Initial purchase $50,000, annual cash payout $2,000 ⇒ headline 4% cash distribution ratio.","Hypothetical earned operating cash part $1,200 ⇒ 2.4% of principal; return-of-capital part $800 ⇒ 1.6% of principal.","If tax treatment confirms return of capital, adjusted tax basis may decline by $800 under applicable law.","A redemption cap or suspension can prevent recovery of the requested cash at the stated sponsor NAV.","No dollar amount from a redemption request should be booked as liquid cash until the contract and execution support it."],"practice":[["What does a 4% distribution from a non-traded REIT fail to establish?","It does not prove four percent earnings or total investment return; some cash may come from borrowings or returned investor capital while market liquidity is constrained."],["Why is a sponsor-reported NAV not a guarantee an investor can withdraw all value on demand?","Non-traded redemption plans can be limited, delayed, repriced or suspended, and the sponsor's valuation may not be an executable market quote."]]},{"title":"Annuities: insurance contracts, income rights and the accumulation/payout split","body":["An annuity is an insurance contract that can support accumulation, retirement income or both. A fixed deferred annuity credits interest under contract guarantees and insurer conditions. A variable annuity exposes some account values to investment subaccounts, usually alongside insurer charges and optional benefit terms. An indexed annuity credits interest by a stated index-linked formula with participation, caps, spreads or floors; it is not equivalent to directly owning an index mutual fund. Insurer financial strength and exact policy language determine promises.","During accumulation, contract owners may contribute money and accumulate value. Annuitization converts an eligible value into a stream of income based on selected payout option and life or term contingencies. Choosing life-only versus life-with-period-certain or joint-life survivor options can change monthly benefit and what heirs may receive after death. Annuitization may eliminate the owner's ability to withdraw an ordinary account balance or change the payment design. A projected payout illustration is not interchangeable with a guaranteed surrender value.","Consider a $100,000 annuity payout illustration promising a hypothetical $600/month for life under a specified annuitant and contract. Annual cash payout is $7,200 or 7.2% of purchase premium, but that is not a 7.2% investment yield. The stream can include return of the original premium, and the economic value depends on lifespan, insurer performance, applicable survivor guarantee, inflation and what amount remains payable upon death. Comparing the payout ratio with a Treasury yield is mathematically and contractually invalid.","Annuities can have a legitimate role for longevity-risk transfer when an individual wishes to reduce the risk of outliving income, but a product's value must be compared with its surrender restrictions, tax benefits, counterparty risk, fees and the household's existing pension or Social Security income. An annuity bought with retirement-account money may not add distinct tax deferral because the underlying account already has such treatment."],"worked":["Annuity premium $100,000 with fictional fixed payout $600/month ⇒ $7,200 annual receipts.","Annual cash payment divided by premium $7,200/$100,000=7.2% payout rate.","That 7.2% is not a yield: principal recovery may be included in each payment.","Life-only and survivor payout options produce different income and beneficiary outcomes.","The financial decision requires insurer guarantee, inflation and liquidity assessment."],"practice":[["Why should a retirement annuity paying 7.2% of initial premium annually not be advertised as a guaranteed 7.2% investment return?","The payment stream often includes return of the original premium and depends on survival, contract options and insurer performance, unlike a coupon yield on principal."],["What is a potential tradeoff when converting a deferred annuity into irreversible lifetime payments?","Longevity income protection can improve while control of the account balance and access for emergency withdrawals or heirs may diminish under selected terms."]]},{"title":"Fees, surrender periods, riders and annuity withdrawal taxes","body":["Variable and some fixed or indexed annuities can have surrender charges, contract administration fees, mortality-and-expense charges, underlying fund expenses and optional rider fees. Some contract charges depend on premium or amount withdrawn; others accrue as a percentage of assets. Advertised credited performance or income guarantees may apply only to a separate notional benefit base used to calculate a payout, not freely withdrawable cash value. A rider showing an increasing 'income base' can be mistaken for actual investment account growth.","Take a fictional variable annuity account worth $20,000 with annual mortality-and-expense charge 1.25%, administration fee 0.15% and underlying investment fund fee 0.80%. Simplified total stated annual percentage charges are 2.20%, or about $440 on an unchanged $20,000 base, before additional rider and transaction fees. This does not predict exact daily fee deduction but reveals the economic drag. A low-cost diversified fund with no annuity benefits is not contractually identical; compare on equal terms.","Suppose $10,000 annuity cash value is being surrendered during a year with a 7% applicable surrender charge on the full withdrawal. Simplified proceeds before tax are $9,300. If a free-withdrawal allowance applies, the chargeable amount may be smaller under actual policy terms. Withdrawals can have ordinary income tax implications, and some early distributions can cause additional tax under federal rules. Tax treatment depends on qualified versus nonqualified status, basis and payout method.","The pre-purchase audit includes free-look period, surrender schedule, market value adjustment, caps and indexing formula, rider conditions, guaranteed versus nonguaranteed values, insurer financial strength, agent compensation and actual beneficiary provisions. A product with large surrender charges is especially unsuitable for a home fund that may be needed earlier than expected. Tax deferral alone rarely justifies ignoring legal liquidity limitations."],"worked":["Hypothetical annual fees 1.25% M&E + 0.15% administration + 0.80% underlying fund =2.20%.","On unchanged $20,000 account base, approximated annual cost $440, excluding other fees.","Full surrender $10,000 with illustrative 7% charge ⇒ pre-tax cash $9,300.","A rider's stated benefit base is not necessarily withdrawable account value.","Federal income tax and additional-tax eligibility require contract-specific classification and age information."],"practice":[["Why can the growth of an annuity 'income benefit base' fail to represent immediately accessible investment wealth?","The benefit base may be a notional value used to calculate specified income guarantees, separate from cash value and subject to rider conditions."],["What information is missing from a salesperson's promise of tax-deferred annuity growth at 6%?","Guaranteed versus illustrated crediting, all contract and underlying fees, surrender rules, taxes, insurer credit, rider terms and whether the quoted base is actually withdrawable."]]},{"title":"Private funds, alternative investments and lockup financing risk","body":["Alternative investments include private equity, private credit, venture capital, hedge funds, real assets and many other structures. The label does not identify one stable asset class, and their returns may reflect leverage, fees, illiquidity, valuation practices and economic cycle risk. Some private placements are limited to qualified or accredited investors or other specific exemptions and may have resale restrictions. The SEC warns that private placements can provide less public reporting, limited liquidity and the possibility of total loss.","Private funds can require capital commitments rather than immediate full payment. A limited partner who commits $50,000 but initially funds only $15,000 may remain legally obligated to contribute another $35,000 when the manager issues capital calls under the partnership agreement. This unfunded commitment is a real future liquidity need even if the account dashboard initially shows only invested capital. Failure to fund can trigger penalties or forfeiture under contract terms.","Fee waterfalls can be complicated. A simplified fund charging 2% annually on a hypothetical $100,000 fee base and 20% of $10,000 investment profit, with no hurdle, catch-up or other adjustments, could incur $2,000 management fee plus $2,000 performance allocation before other costs. Real agreements may compute fee on commitments, invested capital or NAV and use preferred returns, loss carryforwards and high-water marks. Quoted gross IRR may differ dramatically from net investor cash-on-cash return after fees and delayed distributions.","A household with $250–$500 monthly discretionary savings and a future home purchase must not assume private assets can be sold to meet a surprise tax or mortgage bill. Read audited financials, valuation policies, conflicts, side letters, limited-partner rights, capital call schedules and transfer restrictions. The appropriate answer may be to decline an investment for lack of liquidity or verifiable disclosure rather than chase a headline 15% target return."],"worked":["Private fund total commitment $50,000, initial capital call $15,000 ⇒ unfunded obligation $35,000.","Simple annual management fee 2% on $100,000 stated fee base ⇒ $2,000.","Simplified performance fee 20% of eligible $10,000 profit ⇒ $2,000 before actual waterfall adjustments.","Gross performance and net investor return differ with fees, timing and capital calls.","A commitment is not the same as cash already invested or unrestricted liquidity."],"practice":[["Why is a private fund's unfunded capital commitment important to a household's cash budget?","The agreement may legally require additional contributions at manager-selected dates, potentially conflicting with mortgage, tax or emergency obligations."],["Why must '2 and 20' marketing be verified in the actual limited-partnership agreement?","Management fee bases, eligible profit, hurdle and high-water marks, fee timing and additional expenses differ and determine actual net investor returns."]]},{"title":"Alternatives versus home equity, inheritance and portfolio risk policy","body":["Long-horizon alternatives may diversify some traditional public-market exposures, but illiquidity is not automatically a premium the investor will earn. Reported private-asset NAV can be smoothed by periodic appraisal and stale valuations, which may make measured historical volatility look artificially low despite underlying economic risk. High fees, leverage, correlated property markets, manager conflicts and restricted exits can erode or delay returns. A public REIT can be marketable but volatile; a non-traded REIT can be difficult to exit; an annuity may have defined payout guarantees with irreversible elections.","Consider a household holding $18,000 total accessible cash and needing $6,000 emergency reserve plus $12,000 future home closing. A promoter offers a $5,000 nontraded REIT or a $5,000 annuity with surrender limitations. Funding it from the $18,000 leaves only $13,000 accessible, a $5,000 hole against the two stated cash needs. A 4% distribution on the investment produces $200 hypothetical annual receipts, not the $5,000 liquidity required for a closing. A beneficiary trust cannot create extra liquid assets simply by changing legal titles.","An integrated decision compares five dimensions: legal claim, underlying cash source, current executable exit price, time to exit and stress loss. Cash deposits, Treasury bills, index funds, REIT shares, annuities and private funds can each support specific goals, but not one universal safety or performance ranking. Long-term investments should be evaluated after sufficient liquidity is secured and after existing mortgage, insurance and retirement account responsibilities are understood.","Close Module 9 with a comprehensive investment committee memorandum. Identify which product actually solves a stated problem, verify its primary disclosure documents, quantify fee drag and principal stress, and document conditions for choosing none of the products. A plan that preserves home closing and emergency reserves may be more successful than one presenting the highest advertised gross return."],"worked":["Accessible assets $18,000 minus required emergency $6,000 minus home closing $12,000 ⇒ $0 spare cash.","Locking $5,000 into illiquid asset reduces accessible cash to $13,000, shortfall $5,000.","4% distribution on $5,000 = $200/year, not recovery of $5,000 closing principal.","Beneficiary ownership changes do not eliminate illiquid product redemption restrictions.","Suitable investment decisions begin with cash liabilities and documented product rights."],"practice":[["Why can an illiquid alternative investment worsen a family inheritance plan even if it produces cash distributions?","Restricted exits, uncertain valuations, fees and ongoing obligations can leave heirs or a trustee unable to fund mortgage, tax or living expenses when due."],["Why can 'no investment in this product' be the correct conclusion of a professional portfolio assessment?","When contractual costs, unverifiable valuation, complex payouts or cash-locking terms exceed a household's risk and liquidity capacity, abstaining preserves financial resilience."]]}],"sourceRefs":[["SEC Investor.gov Public REITs","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/general-resources/news-alerts/alerts-bulletins/investor-bulletins-65"],["SEC Investor.gov Nontraded REITs","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/general-resources/news-alerts/alerts-bulletins/investor-bulletins-89"],["SEC Investor.gov Annuities","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities"],["SEC Investor.gov Variable Annuity Fees","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/annuities/variable-annuities"],["NAIC Fixed Deferred Annuity Buyer Guide","https://content.naic.org/sites/default/files/publication-anb-lp-consumer-annuities.pdf"],["SEC Investor.gov Private Placement Risks","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/general-resources/news-alerts/alerts-bulletins/investor-bulletins/private-placements-under-regulation-d-updated-investor-bulletin"],["SEC Private Funds Overview","https://www.sec.gov/resources-small-businesses/capital-raising-building-blocks/starting-private-fund"],["IRS Publication 550","https://www.irs.gov/publications/p550"]],"assessment":{"title":"Public and nontraded REIT, annuity and private-fund suitability audit","prompt":"Compute holding-period total return on a $50,000 REIT earning a $2,000 distribution while ending equity value falls to $43,000; derive illustrative FFO ($20m GAAP profit+$15m real estate depreciation−$5m gain) and assess $25m distributions after $10m recurring capital need. Analyze nontraded REIT payout split $1,200 earnings/$800 investor capital. Compare $100,000 annuity $600/month income payout, 2.2% combined annual fees and 7% surrender charge; explain payout versus investment yield. Audit a $50,000 private commitment with $35,000 unfunded obligation. Recommend an appropriate use, or no use, for an investor needing $18,000 near-term home and emergency cash.","rubric":["REIT claim, FFO and liquidity analysis (25%)","Economic distribution and total return distinctions (20%)","Annuity payout, fees and surrender-contract rights (25%)","Private placement obligations, fees and disclosure (20%)","Household suitability and evidence (10%)"]}}],"capstone":{"title":"Connecticut Homebuyer and Retirement Investment Product Committee","intro":"Rigorous fictional portfolio and legal-product selection across bonds, deposits, funds, REITs, annuities and private assets. All interest and return assumptions are illustrations, not current market quotes. The committee must defend liquidity and ownership rights before optimizing yield.","scenario":["A household has $18,000 in accessible money, needs $6,000 emergency liquidity at any time, and expects a $12,000 home-closing payment in twelve months. New monthly saving may be $250, but its continuity is uncertain.","A quoted annual-pay three-year bond has $1,000 face and $40 coupon with hypothetical 5% required market yield. A separately observed bond is acquired at $950, pays a $40 coupon and is sold at $960 one year later.","An illustrative bond fund contains $20,000 market value and modified duration six and suffers an instantaneous 75-basis-point parallel market yield increase. A separate corporate security yields 6% against 4% comparable US Treasury yield with model annual default probability 3% and 60% severity.","One qualifying individual holds $190,000 savings and $100,000 CD deposits at the same FDIC insured bank in the same single-ownership category, with no other eligible complications. A hypothetical $10,000 bank CD earns 4% APY over one year, but a mid-term early withdrawal might carry a 90-day modeled interest penalty.","A Treasury bill acquired for $9,800 pays face $10,000 on maturity after about twenty-six weeks. A separate savings balance $10,000 earns 4% taxable annual interest; assume illustration federal tax on interest 22% and 3% inflation.","A fund with $10m assets, $50,000 liabilities and one million shares quotes NAV. An ETF with $100 NAV displays ask $100.50 and bid $99.90. Two otherwise equal funds charge 0.10% and 1.00% annually on initial $20,000, with assumed 6% gross deterministic return over twenty years.","Two $10,000 funds each contain overlap in the same issuer group: 40% of A and 60% of B. A portfolio with $12,000 equity ETF and $8,000 bond ETF faces −25% and −5% respective market shocks.","A separately modeled publicly traded REIT is bought for $50,000, pays $2,000 distributions in one year and ends worth $43,000. Another REIT reports $20m GAAP income, $15m property depreciation, a $5m sale gain and $10m recurring property cash spending, then pays $25m distributions.","A nontraded REIT offers $2,000 cash distribution; issuer disclosure indicates $1,200 cash generated by operations and $800 returning investor capital under the fictional treatment. Redemption requests may be limited or suspended.","An annuity insurance illustration uses $100,000 purchase premium and $600/month life-contingent pension-style payout. A separately modeled variable annuity has $20,000 account base, 1.25% M&E, .15% administration and .80% fund fees; withdrawing $10,000 during a 7% full surrender charge triggers cash reduction before tax.","A private fund investor commits $50,000, supplies $15,000 at initial capital call and owes up to $35,000 of future commitments according to agreement. Separate illustrative 2% management fee and 20% performance allocation have terms not specified by marketing.","The household considers purchasing a $5,000 illiquid nontraded product from its $18,000 cash. Compare how this changes ability to pay the $6,000 emergency and $12,000 closing obligation."],"tasks":["Explain the coupon, current yield, market yield, maturity claim, and what changes if the debt contract is sold before maturity.","Price the three-year coupon cash flows at 5%, calculate the realized one-year return on a $950 purchase and $960 sale after coupon.","Compute bond fund duration shock, credit spread in basis points and stylized issuer expected credit loss, and identify missing real-world factors.","Compute basic FDIC single-ownership aggregated deposit exposure and explain why a money market mutual fund is not an MMDA bank deposit.","Calculate 4% APY CD one-year cash, the simplified 90-day early withdrawal penalty and 26-week T-bill holding period gain and approximate annualization.","Calculate federal-after-tax and real savings balance at 4% interest, 22% illustrative tax on interest and 3% inflation, and compare tax treatment with Treasury obligations.","Calculate fund NAV, ETF ask premium and bid-ask round-trip loss, then fund expense ratio initial fee gap and compounded long-run return gap.","Explain tracking difference versus tracking error, holdings overlap, and the economic downside on the 60/40 risky portfolio.","Compute REIT distribution ratio and total holding-period return with falling share value; reconcile hypothetical FFO, adjusted cash and distribution gap.","Explain how nontraded REIT cash can include returned investor principal and why an announced redemption plan does not establish immediate liquidity.","Distinguish annuity payout rate from earned yield; calculate model fee drag and surrender cash and explain contract rights and survivor provisions.","Model private fund remaining capital-call obligations and fee waterfall caveats; assess liquidity stress for the $18,000 household.","Propose a cash-preservation allocation, a separate long-term fund allocation if future surplus exists, and evidence checklist citing FDIC, NCUA, SEC, IRS, TreasuryDirect and NAIC."],"answers":["Bond coupon is a contractual stated payment and current yield compares annual coupon with purchase price; YTM discounts all promised flows at acquisition price under performance assumptions, not a guaranteed realized result.","Coupon bond price $40/1.05+$40/1.05²+$1040/1.05³≈$972.77. One-year sale return ($960−$950+$40)/$950≈5.2632%, distinct from current yield $40/$950≈4.2105%.","Duration-six +.0075 rate shock implies −4.5%, about −$900 on $20,000. Credit yield spread 6−4=2 percentage points or 200 bps. Model expected annual credit loss 3%×60%=1.8% of principal absent other complexities.","Same bank and single-ownership category FDIC aggregation $190k+$100k=$290k; basic $250k standard insured limit leaves $40k excess. MMF is a security rather than insured bank money market deposit account.","CD 4% APY on $10k yields $400 for one year under model, before tax. Hypothetical 90-day 4% simple-rate penalty on $10k≈$98.63. T-bill holding gain ($10,000−$9,800)/$9,800≈2.0408% across about 26 weeks; annualization requires rate convention and actual days.","Tax on $400 interest at assumed 22%=$88; after-tax $10,312 nominal. Inflation-adjusted current-unit value=$10,312/1.03≈$10,011.65. US Treasury interest is generally federal taxable but exempt from state/local income tax.","Fund NAV=($10m−$50k)/1m=$9.95. Ask premium ($100.50/$100−1)=0.5%. Bid/ask round-trip loss ($100.50−$99.90)/$100.50≈0.597%. First-year expense difference on $20k between 1% and .1%= $180; at modeled 6% gross approximate net returns 5% and 5.9%, compute $20k×1.05²⁰ versus $20k×1.059²⁰.","Overlapping group $4k+$6k=$10k or half of $20k. Stressed 60/40 portfolio: $12k×.75+$8k×.95=$16,600, loss $3,400 or 17%. Tracking difference is return gap; tracking error needs time-series volatility of relative return.","REIT cash distribution 4% but total return ($43k+$2k−$50k)/$50k=−10%. Simplified FFO $20m+$15m−$5m=$30m; after $10m recurring capital estimate $20m proxy; $25m distributions exceed by $5m.","Nontraded REIT $2,000 distribution includes hypothetical $1,200 earned operations and $800 returned investor capital. A sponsor NAV/redemption plan does not guarantee the full investment can be sold at will.","Annuity $600×12/$100,000=7.2% payout ratio, not earned investment yield. Annual illustrative charges (1.25+.15+.80)%×$20,000=$440. Simple surrender $10k×(1−.07)=$9,300 before taxes/other contract exceptions.","Private commitment $50k with $15k paid leaves $35k potential capital calls. Locking $5k from household's fully allocated $18k leaves $13k accessible and a $5k near-term shortfall; $200 annual income under a 4% cash distribution does not restore liquidity.","Recommended class assignment must prioritize required immediately accessible funds and the matching twelve-month home fund; only surplus not needed for dated obligations should be considered for risk/illiquid assets, subject to actual contracts and tax documentation."],"rubric":["Bond contract, yield and duration mathematics (20%)","Deposit, CD, Treasury and real after-tax cash mechanics (20%)","ETF, fund, expense and diversification analysis (20%)","REIT, annuity and private fund legal cash-flow risk (25%)","Homebuyer suitability, evidence and ethical recommendations (15%)"],"sources":[["SEC Investor.gov Bond Basics","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/bonds-or-fixed-income-products/bonds"],["FDIC Deposit Protection","https://www.fdic.gov/resources/deposit-insurance/brochures/deposits-at-a-glance"],["TreasuryDirect Bills","https://www.treasurydirect.gov/marketable-securities/treasury-bills/"],["Investor.gov Funds","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/investing-basics/investment-products/mutual-funds-and-exchange-traded-funds"],["SEC Nontraded REITs","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/general-resources/news-alerts/alerts-bulletins/investor-bulletins-89"],["NAIC Annuities Guide","https://content.naic.org/sites/default/files/publication-anb-lp-consumer-annuities.pdf"],["SEC Private Placements Risk","https://www.investor.gov/introduction-investing/general-resources/news-alerts/alerts-bulletins/investor-bulletins/private-placements-under-regulation-d-updated-investor-bulletin"],["IRS Investments Publication 550","https://www.irs.gov/publications/p550"]]},"rules":{"preserveOriginalChapters":true,"preserveStateKey":true,"assumedRatesNotMarketQuotes":true,"privateModelsDoNotPersist":true,"noAccreditationClaim":true}};
